WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.560
Since 1984, Wes Craven's A Nightmare on Elm
Street has sent shockwaves through pop culture,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.660
mostly thanks to the unkillable psycho villain
Freddy Krueger.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.520
But who was the man behind the makeup wielding
that nightmarish claw?

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.820
His name is Robert Englund, and he's done
much more than just play a quippy child killer

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.840
in increasingly cheesy horror movies.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.490
In real life, Englund is a respected character
actor, and he's been keeping busy since his

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.840
last turn in the role in Freddy vs. Jason.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.160
Here's everything you need to know about the
man behind the monster who haunts your dreams.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.280
Horror never ends

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.040
As Freddy Krueger, Englund's very face became
synonymous with the horror genre.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.080
To this day, he still lands roles in horror
movies around the globe, with a new project,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.280
the puppet-based thriller Abruptio, expected
for release in 2019.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.200
After doing more than 75 movies in his career,
he estimates at least 20 of them are horror.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.260
While it's been nearly two decades since he's
suited up as Freddy, genre projects are always

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.260
there for him when he needs a quick payday.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:02.530
Speaking in a 2013 interview with the Chicago-area
Daily Herald, he said,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:06.021
"When things get slow for me in Hollywood
[...] I can always go off to Europe and do

00:01:06.021 --> 00:01:07.550
a movie in Italy or Spain.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.700
This is the great gift, the happy accident
of my career.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:15.960
Because horror movies, like science fiction
and action and fantasy movies, speak the international

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:16.960
language of cinema."

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.440
Of course, that kind of attitude means that
some movies Englund has shown up in have been

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.520
less than stellar.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.760
He's open about the fact that he'll sometimes
take jobs just for the paycheck.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.060
If you thought he signed on to Strippers vs.
Werewolves for artistic reasons, well, think

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:31.560
again.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.540
Gamer dreams

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:38.120
Englund did more than just voice act for 2010's
Call of Duty: Black Ops.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.640
For a bonus mode in that game, he also showed
up as himself.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.590
Teaming up with Danny Trejo, Sarah Michelle
Gellar, and Michael Rooker, he appeared as

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.420
a playable character in a zombie-themed level
named "Call of the Dead."

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.960
An insane interactive love letter to horror
cinema, the level is a treat for fans of the

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.119
genre who also happen to like playing first-person
shooters.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.300
"Watch out zombies, I'm a comin' for ya!"

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.080
Englund's favorite part about the role was
the reaction he received from the children

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.640
in his family, too young to remember Freddy
Krueger at the height of his powers.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.860
"Freddy's kind of old to them, it's sort of
old school to them, ya know, and uh, now that

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.000
Uncle Robert is working on Call of Duty he's
cool again"

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:21.900
Con jobs

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.680
Some actors grow to resent the fandoms that
pop up around their most famous characters,

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:31.060
but if Englund has any frustration with being
associated with Freddy, he doesn't show it.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:31.560
As he puts it,

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.720
"Freddy will get top billing in my obituary."

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.720
Instead, he makes an effort to meet fans whenever
he's able, particularly around the lucrative

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:39.560
Halloween season.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.940
For years, Englund has generally done three
or four appearances a year, some at Comic

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:49.200
Con-style conventions, and some at specific
screenings of Nightmare on Elm Street movies.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.360
They're good occasions to meet the man behind
the makeup, pose for a photo, or get something

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:53.860
signed.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:58.700
In the movies, messages from Freddy were heart-stopping
moments that portended death; you can have

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.340
a lot more fun with a nice autograph.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.340
Freddy presents...

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.160
Englund also stepped behind the camera, starting
with a horror film called 976-EVIL, which

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.520
was released in 1988 while he was still making
movies as the dreaded Fred Krueger.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.120
Years later, he also directed the 2008 horror
comedy Killer Pad.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.420
Beyond those original projects, Englund has
also taken a retrospective look at his own

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.620
career, starring in and helping to produce
Nightmares in the Makeup Chair, a documentary

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.360
about the long hours of work it takes to bring
Freddy to life through practical makeup effects.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.760
In a statement about the movie, Englund called
the doc,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.240
"[...] my love letter to the Nightmare on
Elm Street series and to practical makeup."

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.200
"YOU'RE ALL MY CHILDREN NOW, PIGGIES.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.840
HAHAHAHAHA!"

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.580
Reboot rebuttal

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.600
While the quality of each installment varies,
the Nightmare On Elm Street movies all possess

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.900
a certain charm, except, of course, for the
2010 remake.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:57.700
Critics were cold to the overly-serious treatment
of the material, and so was the actor who

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:58.800
made the films iconic.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:03.100
While he had nothing but kind things to say
about the reboot's cast, Englund says the

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:07.439
remake missed the mark, partly by going in
a much darker direction than series fans were

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:07.940
used to.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.540
In his opinion, the movie needed a happy-go-lucky
element.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.780
"I think that that ingredient was stepped
on by their reshoots which kind of set it

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.260
deeper into the curse of Freddy by the time
the movie began."

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.480
Guest star Robert Englund

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.351
Even though his most famous role caked him
behind layers of monstrous makeup, Englund

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.620
is still effective as a plain-faced character
actor.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.560
It's work he's kept up steadily following
the end of his involvement in the Nightmare

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:38.350
franchise, appearing in shows like Hawaii
5-0, Supernatural, and Criminal Minds, with

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:43.380
guest spots often playing off his notoriety
as the infamous Springwood Slasher.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.040
While Englund's personal nightmare may be
over, the man of your dreams is still with

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.720
us, still working, and never too far away
from prime time.

